ing. It's also possible that one is failing because of the other. Rolling back the wrong service would change production without fixing the problem, so I held off on the rollback. I also held off on the incident, because it gets filed against one specific service.\n\nThere are no open incidents yet. Which would you like?\n1. Roll back `payments-api` to v5.0.0\n2. Roll back `payments-worker` to v3.3.0\n3. Roll back both\n\nOnce you choose, I'll do the rollback right away, check that the service is healthy again, and open a SEV2 incident for it. If you want someone paged now, I can open the SEV2 first and name both services in it.", "type": "text"}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"id": "toolu_01KpvaaiDF9HgXZRzQcYE7L6", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-worker", "version": "v3.3.0"}, "name": "rollback", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}, {"id": "toolu_01KsPSFYETWuFLjkKPJ9BP1L", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"title": "payments-worker errors after v3.4.0 deploy; rolled back to v3.3.0", "severity": "SEV2", "service": "payments-worker", "summary": "payments-worker v3.4.0 (deployed this morning) became unhealthy in all production regions (us-east, us-west, eu-west), with an 18% error rate. payments-api (v5.1.0) is also degraded at 18% errors, which the user says is downstream of the worker. Action taken: payments-worker rolled back to v3.3.0, its last release that was healthy in production. Follow-up: confirm the worker and payments-api recover, and find the root cause in v3.4.0.", "idempotency_key": "payments-worker-v3.4.0-rollback-sev2"}, "name": "create_incident", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"id": "toolu_01KJ467LGSNUVPGJ7H5MA6vG", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-worker"}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}, {"id": "toolu_016PPgWGiXnmkUK66PgyLp7S", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-api"}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I rolled `payments-worker` back to v3.3.0 and opened a SEV2. The worker has recovered, but `payments-api` is still failing.\n\n- **Rollback:** a human approver signed off on it, and v3.3.0 is now running in us-east, us-west and eu-west. That's the worker's last release that was healthy in production.\n- **Worker:** it now reports healthy, and its error rate dropped from 18% to 0.2%.\n- **Incident:** INC-0001 is open as a SEV2 against `payments-worker`, and on-call has been paged. The incident notes say the API errors are downstream of the worker.\n- **API still failing:** right after the rollback, `payments-api` (still on v5.1.0) was unhealthy at an 18% error rate, unchanged from before. It may just need time to recover. But if it doesn't, the worker may not be the only cause. The API's own v5.1.0 release this morning also went from healthy to degraded.\n\nI haven't touched `payments-api`. I'd recheck it in a few minutes. If it's still failing, rolling it back to v5.0.0, its last healthy release, is the next step. Do you want me to recheck it or do that rollback now?", "type": "text"}]}]}